  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nToday through tomorrow night...\n\nLatest night fog reveals that there are some areas of low patchy \nclouds out over the inner waters of the SOCAL bight, yet the coastal \nareas remain clear themselves at this hour, and will likely remain \nthat way through the morning as there are still a substantial amount \nof mid to upper level clouds moving over the region. There are still \na few lingering showers, although the majority of the rain has now \ncome to an end as the moisture associated with a decaying \nTropical Storm Marie well to our southwest continues to move west \nout of the area. Skies will begin to clear later in the day. The \nridge in place will further build going into the middle of the \nweek. Coincidentally, a warming trend will also take place, with \nhighs returning to above average temperatures by tomorrow. For the\novernight hours, there will be a struggling marine layer \nattempting to form and establish itself, and there could be areas \nof patchy fog developing within some of the higher wind-sheltered \nvalleys, especially where there is antecedent soil moisture from \nrecent rainfall. Lows will still be balmy as well, with humidity \nstill being above average. Tomorrow will bring about further \nwarming and drying.\n\nWednesday through Sunday...\n\nAs the ridge continues to build into the middle of the week, \ntemperatures will warm back above the seasonal average, with highs \npeaking on Wednesday, which will find high temperatures returning \nback into the 90s for much of the inland areas, and back over 100 \nwithin the IE. The deserts will also see temperatures reaching back \ninto the 110s. This, in combination with humidity will produce some \nhigh heat indices for the lower elevations of the deserts. There \nwill still be moisture in place that will move up clockwise around \nthe high pressure centered over the Four Corners area that will keep \na slight chance of afternoon showers and thunderstorms for the \nmountains and adjacent deserts, along with a gradual cooldown \nthroughout the rest of the work week, especially on Wednesday and\nThursday during the afternoon hours. From there, troughing is \ngoing to help displace mid level moisture further to the east and \nsuppress the chance of afternoon showers and thunderstorms for \nthe mountains and deserts. From there, further cooling will occur \ngoing into the following weekend with drier and breezier \nconditions. The marine layer will likely also continue to be \ndisplaced and patchy throughout the rest of the work week, and \nthen should become more prevalent thereafter going into the \nweekend as better onshore flow returns.",
